  A4 -- where the isolated Route A anomaly PEAKS, and where the external field takes over
  With u = sqrt(y) = r_M/r and g_N = a_0 u^2, the anomaly is dg = a_0 u^2/(e^u - 1).  Maximising gives
  2(e^u - 1) = u e^u, i.e. e^u (2 - u) = 2.  (The naive answer u = 2 drops the 1/(e^u-1) denominator and
  is wrong by 25% in radius -- the numerical argmax below is what caught it.)
   canonical: u* = 1.593624 (y* = 2.5396)  ->  r_peak =  4994.7 AU, dg_peak = 6.062e-11 m/s^2 = 0.6476 a_0   [numerical argmax  4994.7 AU, 0.0004% off]
              r_M = sqrt(GM/a0) =   7960 AU;  external field takes over at r_ext = sqrt(GM/g_ext) =  5257 AU;  g_ext/g_N at the peak = 0.90
         alt: u* = 1.593624 (y* = 2.5396)  ->  r_peak =  4545.7 AU, dg_peak = 7.318e-11 m/s^2 = 0.6476 a_0   [numerical argmax  4545.8 AU, 0.0012% off]
              r_M = sqrt(GM/a0) =   7244 AU;  external field takes over at r_ext = sqrt(GM/g_ext) =  5257 AU;  g_ext/g_N at the peak = 0.75
  [PASS] A4a  the anomaly peaks at the exact root of e^u(2-u)=2, at ~5000 AU, and never exceeds ~7e-11 m/s^2   (analytic u* = 1.5936 confirmed by numerical argmax to 0.0004%.  Peak dg = 6.06e-11 m/s^2 = 0.648 a_0 at 4995 AU (canonical).  THIS IS THE CEILING ON THE WHOLE EFFECT: no heliocentric distance, at any eccentricity, on either footing, produces a larger anomalous acceleration than 7.32e-11 m/s^2.  Caveat carried forward: the external field is already 0.90 of the solar field at the peak (crossover 5257 AU), so the isolated calculation OVERSTATES the peak by up to a factor ~2 (doubling y raises sqrt(y) by 1.41 and divides nu-1 by ~1.9) and is INVALID beyond ~5257 AU -- the Oort cloud is EFE-governed, which is the regime h12_h14_satellites_and_oort.py covers, not this one)

  A5 -- THE PART THE EXPONENTIAL TAIL DOES NOT SAVE: the external-field quadrupole Q_2
  Q_2 is NOT a property of the field at Saturn.  It is the constant tidal quadrupole that the nonlinear
  Poisson equation's external boundary condition at r_M ~ 8000 AU imprints on the WHOLE interior.  There
  y ~ 1, so there is no exponential suppression and Route A inherits the standard MOND-class value.
  Desmond, Hees & Famaey 2024 (arXiv:2401.04796) eq (12):
     q = (3/2) Int_0^inf dv Int_-1^1 dxi (nu-1)[eN(3xi-5xi^3) + v^2(1-3xi^2)],  nu at sqrt(eN^2+v^4+2 eN v^2 xi)
  with eN nu(eN) = etilde = g_ext/a_0, and Q_2 = -(3 a_0^{3/2})/(2 sqrt(GM)) q.  Their published Fig. 1
  anchors are quoted for nu_RAR(y) = 1/(1-exp(-sqrt y)) -- which IS Route A's kernel, so this is a direct
  published pin with no shape assumption imported.

