----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PART B -- the discriminant: anomalous perihelion precession as an exact function of (a, e)
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  In modified GRAVITY the correction is central, so the precession follows from the force alone.  To first
  order, Gauss's form of the Lagrange planetary equations for a purely radial perturbing acceleration,
  integrated over one orbit in true anomaly f:

      Delta(omega) = (1/(GM e)) Int_0^{2pi} dg(r(f)) r(f)^2 cos f  df ,      r = a(1-e^2)/(1+e cos f)

  with dg > 0 meaning EXTRA INWARD.  A pure function of (a, e) and of nothing else: no trajectory memory,
  no dependence on where the orbit has been.  THAT is the modified-gravity arm's content, and it is what a
  trajectory-dependent modification must violate.

  B1 -- the integrator, validated against two independent closed forms with opposite signs
  GR      a=   0.3871 AU e=0.2056: analytic +5.018660439e-07  numeric +5.018660439e-07  rel -1.44e-15
  GR      a=   9.5367 AU e=0.0539: analytic +1.956638855e-08  numeric +1.956638855e-08  rel -2.55e-15
  GR      a= 506.2000 AU e=0.8496: analytic +1.321289992e-09  numeric +1.321289992e-09  rel -3.11e-15
  const   a=   0.3871 AU e=0.2056: analytic -1.553751820e-11  numeric -1.553751820e-11  rel +2.00e-15
  const   a=   9.5367 AU e=0.0539: analytic -9.622373921e-09  numeric -9.622373921e-09  rel -2.22e-16
  const   a= 506.2000 AU e=0.8496: analytic -1.431944437e-05  numeric -1.431944437e-05  rel +2.22e-16
  [PASS] B1a  the precession integrator reproduces the GR closed form 6 pi GM/(c^2 a(1-e^2))   (worst relative error 3.11e-15 over e = 0.054 to 0.850, a = 0.39 to 506 AU)
  [PASS] B1b  and the constant-sunward closed form -2 pi dg a^2 sqrt(1-e^2)/GM used for the Part A bounds   (worst relative error 2.00e-15.  Note the SIGN: a constant extra inward force precesses the perihelion RETROGRADE, because it falls off slower than 1/r^2, while the GR 1/r^4 term is prograde.  Two closed forms with opposite signs and different r-powers, both reproduced -- the integrator is not tuned to either)

  B2 -- MUTATION CONTROL on the integrator: a perturbation that MUST give exactly zero
  A dg proportional to 1/r^2 is nothing but a rescaling of GM.  It cannot precess anything, and if the
  integrator carried a spurious constant it would show up here as a fake precession.  A 1/r^3 term of the
  same amplitude is the reference that shows the integrator is not simply returning zero.
  a=   1.00 AU e=0.3000:  1/r^2 (pure GM rescale) -1.881e-26   1/r^3 reference +5.822e-11   ratio 3.23e-16
  a= 506.20 AU e=0.8496:  1/r^2 (pure GM rescale) -7.229e-27   1/r^3 reference +3.762e-13   ratio 1.92e-14
  [PASS] B2a  MUTATION CONTROL: a 1/r^2 perturbation gives exactly zero precession, a 1/r^3 one does not   (the 1/r^2 case returns 1.9e-14 of the 1/r^3 reference.  This is a THEOREM as well as a control, and Part D uses it: any modification that merely rescales the effective GM along an orbit is invisible to perihelion precession, no matter how large it is)

  B3 -- THE DISCRIMINANT FUNCTION: modified-gravity precession at fixed a, circular vs eccentric
  Delta(omega) per orbit and the rate in mas/yr, for e = 0.01 and e = 0.90 at the same semi-major axis.
  The ratio is the modified-gravity arm's OWN eccentricity signature: it exists entirely because the
  eccentric orbit's aphelion samples a lower acceleration.  Canonical footing; alt differs only in scale.
  Everything is in log10 because the values underflow float64 inside ~30 AU.  The last column flags rows
  where the galactic external field at aphelion exceeds 30% of the Sun's (crossover r_ext = 5257 AU), so
  this isolated calculation stops being valid -- those rows are shown for shape only, not as predictions.
