  [PASS] B3a  modified gravity DOES carry a huge eccentricity signature -- in ratio, though not in magnitude   (at a = 10 AU the e=0.9 precession exceeds the e=0.01 one by 1.1e+157, because aphelion at 19 AU sits at sqrt(y) = 419 against 796 at 10 AU and the kernel is exp(-sqrt y).  So it is NOT true that modified gravity is eccentricity-blind: the CENTRAL FORCE is blind, the ORBIT AVERAGE is not.  Recorded because the naive statement of the fork gets this backwards)
  [PASS] B3b  but the discriminant is vacuous inside 100 AU -- the signal is not small, it is absent   (the largest modified-gravity precession rate anywhere inside 100 AU, at the most favourable eccentricity e = 0.9, is 1.9e-14 mas/yr.  The bar here is set by the MEASUREMENT, not by the result: one part in 1e6 of Mars's 0.00037 mas/yr, the sharpest perihelion sigma in astronomy (Pitjeva & Pitjev 2013).  The actual shortfall is 10^10.  No measurement, existing or conceivable, reaches it)

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PART C -- which real objects give the best lever, in (a, e) space
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Three readings of the same kernel are compared on every orbit.  They bracket the fork:
    modified GRAVITY : dg = (nu(y(r)) - 1) g_N(r), keyed to the LOCAL distance      [the field at a point]
    MI-A envelope    : dg = a CONSTANT equal to the modified-gravity value AT APHELION, held all orbit
    MI-B envelope    : nu itself FROZEN at its aphelion value, i.e. dg = (nu(y_apo)-1) g_N(r)
  MI-A and MI-B are UPPER ENVELOPES, deliberately extreme (Milgrom 2009 MNRAS 399 474: MI anomalies attach
  to trajectories that reach low-acceleration regions).  A real nonlocal MI kernel lands between them and
  the modified-gravity curve.  If the most generous reading is unmeasurable, every reading is.
  NOTE, and it is load-bearing: by the B2a theorem MI-B is a pure GM rescale and PRECESSES NOTHING.  It is
  therefore not a competitor in Part B at all; it reappears in D3 as a different observable.

  object              a [AU]        e  q [AU]   Q [AU]  MG log10 dg      at q    MI-A dg   MI-B eps     P [yr]     EFE?
  Mercury               0.39  0.20564    0.31      0.5      -7408.5  -11243.0   0.00e+00   0.00e+00        0.2         
  Saturn                9.54  0.05386    9.02     10.1       -348.2    -387.2   0.00e+00   0.00e+00       29.5         
  Neptune              30.07  0.00859   29.81     30.3       -119.2    -121.1  6.75e-120  1.05e-114      164.9         
  1P/Halley            17.83  0.96714    0.59     35.1       -103.9   -5900.5  1.40e-104   2.91e-99       75.3         
  136199 Eris          67.86  0.43607   38.27     97.5        -41.7     -95.7   2.12e-42   3.39e-36      559.1         
  2012 VP113          266.00  0.69000   82.46    449.5        -15.2     -48.0   6.00e-16   2.04e-08     4338.4         
  90377 Sedna         506.20  0.84960   76.13    936.3        -11.9     -51.4   1.37e-12   2.03e-04    11389.2         
  LPC a=2500 AU      2500.00  0.99800    5.00   4995.0        -10.2    -695.0   6.06e-11   2.55e-01   125002.4  INVALID
  Oort-spike LPC    20000.00  0.99995    1.00  39999.0        -10.8   -3459.0   1.68e-11   4.54e+00  2828480.5  INVALID
    'MG log10 dg' is at aphelion, ' at q' at perihelion -- their difference is how far apart the readings
     sit ON THE SAME ORBIT, which is the entire discriminant.  MI-B eps is dimensionless (a GM fraction).
     'EFE INVALID' marks orbits where the galactic external field at aphelion exceeds 30% of the Sun's
     (crossover r_ext = 5257 AU), so this isolated calculation does not apply -- the two long-period-comet
     rows are there to locate the optimum in (a,e), NOT as predictions.  Sedna's aphelion (936 AU) is at
     g_ext/g_N = 0.032 and is safely inside.
  [PASS] C1a  the lever exists and it is enormous IN RATIO: on Sedna the readings differ by >10^25   (Sedna is observed near perihelion (q = 76 AU) where modified gravity predicts 10^-51 m/s^2 -- exactly nothing -- while an aphelion-keyed modified inertia carries 1.4e-12 m/s^2 around the whole orbit.  A ratio of 10^40.  The fork is not subtle on this object; it is the difference between zero and something.  The whole question is whether the something is measurable)

  C2 -- where in (a, e) the arms differ MOST in ABSOLUTE terms, and what object sits there
  The envelope is maximised when the APHELION sits on the anomaly's peak (A4a).  With e -> 1 that means
  a = r_peak/2: a long-period comet.  Modified gravity's value at such an object's PERIHELION, where it is
  actually observed, is identically zero.  So the theoretical optimum of this entire test IS the
  long-period comet population -- and D1 shows why that optimum is unusable.
   canonical: optimum a =   2497 AU (aphelion on the peak); at q = 5 AU, e = 0.99800: MI-A Delta(omega) = 2.533e-02 rad/orbit = 1.45 deg/orbit, P = 124802 yr
         alt: optimum a =   2273 AU (aphelion on the peak); at q = 5 AU, e = 0.99780: MI-A Delta(omega) = 2.655e-02 rad/orbit = 1.52 deg/orbit, P = 108361 yr
  [PASS] C2a  the theoretical optimum is a ~2500 AU long-period comet carrying ~1 degree per orbit   (1.45 deg/orbit (canonical) / 1.52 (alt) at a = 2497 AU, q = 5 AU.  That is a LARGE number, not a small one -- which is why the shutdown in Part D is a statement about MEASURABILITY, not about the size of the physics.  Recorded as the ceiling any future experiment on this route would be aiming at)

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PART D -- how far short, quantified; and the one channel that is not shut
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  D1 -- the theoretical optimum: killed three times over by comet physics
  outgassing at q = 1 AU: A1 = 1e-08 AU/d^2 -> 2.00e-07 m/s^2  vs the ABSOLUTE peak anomaly 6.06e-11 m/s^2  ->  outgassing is 3306x larger
  outgassing at q = 5 AU: A1 = 1e-08 AU/d^2 -> 8.02e-09 m/s^2  vs the ABSOLUTE peak anomaly 6.06e-11 m/s^2  ->  outgassing is 132x larger
  per-passage planetary scatter in 1/a is ~1e-04 AU^-1 (h12_h14_satellites_and_oort.out): 25% of 1/a for an a = 2497 AU comet
  and a long-period comet is seen for ONE apparition, so a per-orbit secular precession has no second
  epoch to be differenced against.  Three independent kills, any one of which suffices.
  [FAIL] D1a  the long-period-comet optimum is usable: the signal exceeds the outgassing   (IT IS NOT.  A typical A1 = 1e-8 AU/d^2 gives 8.0e-09 m/s^2 at q = 5 AU against the ABSOLUTE CEILING of the anomaly 6.1e-11 m/s^2 -- 132x larger -- and A1 is fitted per comet to 10-30%, so the outgassing UNCERTAINTY ALONE is ~26x the entire signal.  Add one apparition and a 25% per-passage scatter in 1/a and the route is not marginal, it is closed)

  D2 -- Sedna, the best inert lever that exists: the orbit-fit absorption calculation
  Sedna has no outgassing (85 AU, inactive), a ~25-yr observed arc (2001 precovery to 2026), and the
  largest well-determined aphelion of any inert object.  A purely radial perturbation preserves the orbit
  plane, so only the 4 in-plane elements (a, e, omega, M0) are free.  Two observables are used: the
  heliocentric longitude, and ln r -- the latter because a TNO's distance IS measured, by the annual
  parallax, with sigma(ln r) = sigma(theta) * r/AU.  No range measurement is assumed.
  control: a*(1+1e-9) absorbed to 2.61e-06 of itself;  e+1e-9 to 6.16e-07;  integrator-vs-Kepler at dg=0: 5.68e-05 mas
  [PASS] D2a  MUTATION CONTROL: pure element changes are absorbed, and the integrator baseline is clean   (a 1e-9 fractional change in a is absorbed to 2.6e-06 of itself and a 1e-9 change in e to 6.2e-07 -- so the projector is not leaking in-family signal and calling it a detection.  The dg=0 integrator reproduces the analytic Kepler orbit to 5.7e-05 mas, 405x below the smallest signal reported below.  THIS CONTROL EARNED ITS PLACE: the first version of this script used an unnormalised least-squares solve, which truncated a design column and manufactured a 58 mas residual out of a perturbation that is provably degenerate)

     footing   reading   dg or eps   raw [mas]  post-fit [mas]  chi @100mas  chi @10mas
   canonical      MI-A   1.375e-12   3.549e-01       2.263e-02         0.00        0.05
   canonical      MI-B   2.032e-04   1.565e+03       2.749e+00         0.57        5.70
         alt      MI-A   2.952e-12   7.623e-01       4.860e-02         0.01        0.10
         alt      MI-B   4.364e-04   3.360e+03       5.903e+00         1.22       12.24
    raw = the angular signal before any orbit fit; post-fit = what survives the 4-element in-plane fit,
    which is what is actually measurable.  chi is over 401 epochs at the stated per-epoch astrometry.
    100 mas ~ current ground-based for a V=20.6 TNO; 10 mas ~ LSST-class.  BOTH ARE ESTIMATES, not a
    published Sedna orbit covariance, which this repository does not hold.
  [FAIL] D2b  Sedna's 25-yr arc reaches the MI-A precession envelope   (IT DOES NOT, and not remotely.  The aphelion-keyed constant anomaly 1.4e-12 m/s^2 leaves 2.26e-02 mas after the orbit fit: 0.005 sigma at 100 mas and 0.05 sigma even at 10 mas (alt footing 0.010 / 0.10).  THE REASON IS STRUCTURAL, NOT INSTRUMENTAL: 25 yr is 0.22% of Sedna's orbit, and a secular perihelion advance is not defined on a hundredth of an orbit -- the four free elements absorb 94% of the raw signal (a factor 16).  So the PRECESSION channel is shut on the best inert object in the sky)

  D3 -- the one channel that is NOT shut, reported with its killer attached
  MI-B -- nu frozen at the orbit's minimum acceleration -- is exactly a rescaling of the effective GM
  along that orbit.  By the B2a theorem it precesses NOTHING, so it is invisible to Part B entirely.  But
  it is not invisible to an orbit fit that has an independent distance, because the same 4 elements cannot
  simultaneously null the angular and the radial signature.
   canonical: eps = 2.032e-04 (Sedna) = 67.7 M_earth of equivalent interior mass;  post-fit 2.75 mas;  0.57 sigma at 100 mas, 5.70 sigma at 10 mas
         alt: eps = 4.364e-04 (Sedna) = 145.3 M_earth of equivalent interior mass;  post-fit 5.90 mas;  1.22 sigma at 100 mas, 12.24 sigma at 10 mas
  and the CONTRAST that makes it a fork test: modified gravity's eps at Sedna's PRESENT distance (85 AU) is 10^-41, against MI-B's 2.03e-04.
  A ratio of 10^37.  The two arms bracket the
  measurement, so a Sedna effective-GM determination at the 1e-4 level lands INSIDE the bracket.
  [FAIL] D3a  the MI-B effective-GM channel is currently detectable at 3 sigma   (NOT AT PRESENT PRECISION -- 0.57 sigma at 100 mas over 401 epochs -- but it is the ONLY solar-system channel in this script that is within reach at all: 5.7 sigma (canonical) / 12.2 sigma (alt) at 10 mas LSST-class astrometry.  IT IS REPORTED WITH ITS KILLER ATTACHED, and the killer is decisive on the positive side: eps = 2.03e-04 of M_sun is 68 M_earth of unmodelled interior mass, so a DETECTION would be attributed to Planet Nine, not to gravity.  This is the repo's own known-bug pattern -- a residual degenerate with a nuisance parameter -- and it is flagged here rather than discovered later.  ONLY THE NULL SIDE IS CLEAN: a Sedna eps < 1e-4 would exclude the most generous MI reading on a real orbit, which nothing currently does)

